## sn35.239
sn35.239:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, when a mendicant has three qualities they’re full of joy and happiness in this very life, and they have laid the groundwork for ending the defilements. | T: “Endowed with three qualities, a monk dwells full of happiness & joy in the here & now, and has initiated a source for the ending of the effluents.
sn35.239:1.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
sn35.239:1.3 | S: They guard the sense doors, eat in moderation, and are committed to wakefulness. | T: He is one who guards the doors to his sense faculties, knows moderation in eating, & is devoted to wakefulness.
sn35.239:2.1 | S: And how does a mendicant guard the sense doors? | T: “And how is a monk one who guard the doors to his sense faculties?
sn35.239:2.2 | S: When a mendicant sees a sight with their eyes, they don’t get caught up in the features and details. | T: There is the case where a monk, on seeing a form with the eye, doesn’t grasp at any theme or variations by which—
sn35.239:2.3 | S: If the faculty of sight were left unrestrained, bad unskillful qualities of covetousness and displeasure would become overwhelming. For this reason, they practice restraint, protecting the faculty of sight, and achieving its restraint. | T: if he were to dwell without restraint over the faculty of the eye—evil, unskillful qualities such as greed or distress might assail him. He practices with restraint. He guards the faculty of the eye. He achieves restraint with regard to the faculty of the eye.
sn35.239:2.4 | S: When they hear a sound with their ears … | T: “On hearing a sound with the ear…
sn35.239:2.5 | S: When they smell an odor with their nose … | T: “On smelling an aroma with the nose…
sn35.239:2.6 | S: When they taste a flavor with their tongue … | T: “On tasting a flavor with the tongue…
sn35.239:2.7 | S: When they feel a touch with their body … | T: “On touching a tactile sensation with the body…
sn35.239:2.8 | S: When they know an idea with their mind, they don’t get caught up in the features and details. | T: “On cognizing an idea with the intellect, he doesn’t grasp at any theme or variations by which—
sn35.239:2.9 | S: If the faculty of mind were left unrestrained, bad unskillful qualities of covetousness and displeasure would become overwhelming. For this reason, they practice restraint, protecting the faculty of mind, and achieving its restraint. | T: if he were to dwell without restraint over the faculty of the intellect—evil, unskillful qualities such as greed or distress might assail him. He practices with restraint. He guards the faculty of the intellect. He achieves restraint with regard to the faculty of the intellect.
sn35.239:2.10 | S: Suppose a chariot stood harnessed to thoroughbreds at a level crossroads, with a goad ready. A deft horse trainer, a master charioteer, might mount the chariot, taking the reins in his right hand and goad in the left. He’d drive out and back wherever he wishes, whenever he wishes. | T: “Suppose there were a chariot on level ground at four crossroads, harnessed to thoroughbreds, waiting with whips lying ready, so that a dexterous driver, a trainer of tamable horses, might mount and—taking the reins with his left hand and the whip with his right—drive out & back, to whatever place & by whichever road he liked;
sn35.239:2.11 | S: In the same way, a mendicant trains to protect, control, tame, and pacify these six senses. | T: in the same way, the monk trains for the protection of these six senses, for their restraint, for their taming, for their stilling.
sn35.239:2.12 | S: That’s how a mendicant guards the sense doors. | T: “This is how a monk is one who guards the doors to his sense faculties.
sn35.239:3.1 | S: And how does a mendicant eat in moderation? | T: “And how is a monk one who knows moderation in eating?
sn35.239:3.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant reflects rationally on the food that they eat: | T: There is the case where a monk, considering it appropriately, takes his food
sn35.239:3.3 | S: ‘Not for fun, indulgence, adornment, or decoration, but only to sustain this body, to avoid harm, and to support spiritual practice. In this way, I shall put an end to old discomfort and not give rise to new discomfort, and I will have the means to keep going, blamelessness, and a comfortable abiding.’ | T: not playfully, nor for intoxication, nor for putting on bulk, nor for beautification, but simply for the survival & continuance of this body, for ending its afflictions, for the support of the holy life, thinking, ‘I will destroy old feelings [of hunger] & not create new feelings [from overeating]. Thus I will maintain myself, be blameless, & live in comfort.’
sn35.239:3.4 | S: It’s like a person who puts ointment on a wound only so that it can heal; or who oils an axle only so that it can carry a load. | T: “Just as a person anoints a wound simply for its healing, or greases an axle simply for the sake of carrying a load,
sn35.239:3.5 | S: In the same way, a mendicant reflects rationally on the food that they eat: | T: in the same way a monk, considering it appropriately, takes his food
sn35.239:3.6 | S: ‘Not for fun, indulgence, adornment, or decoration, but only to sustain this body, to avoid harm, and to support spiritual practice. In this way, I shall put an end to old discomfort and not give rise to new discomfort, and I will have the means to keep going, blamelessness, and a comfortable abiding.’ | T: not playfully, nor for intoxication, nor for putting on bulk, nor for beautification, but simply for the survival & continuance of this body, for ending its afflictions, for the support of the holy life, thinking, ‘I will destroy old feelings [of hunger] & not create new feelings [from overeating]. Thus I will maintain myself, be blameless, & live in comfort.’
sn35.239:3.7 | S: That’s how a mendicant eats in moderation. | T: “This is how a monk is one who knows moderation in eating.
sn35.239:4.1 | S: And how is a mendicant committed to wakefulness? | T: “And how is a monk one who is devoted to wakefulness?
sn35.239:4.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant practices walking and sitting meditation by day, purifying their mind from obstacles. | T: There is the case where a monk during the day, sitting & pacing back & forth, cleanses his mind of any qualities that would hold the mind in check.
sn35.239:4.3 | S: In the first watch of the night, they continue to practice walking and sitting meditation. | T: During the first watch of the night [dusk to 10 p.m.], sitting & pacing back & forth, he cleanses his mind of any qualities that would hold the mind in check.
sn35.239:4.4 | S: In the middle watch, they lie down in the lion’s posture—on the right side, placing one foot on top of the other—mindful and aware, and focused on the time of getting up. | T: During the second watch of the night [10 p.m. to 2 a.m.], reclining on his right side, he takes up the lion’s posture, one foot placed on top of the other, mindful, alert, with his mind set on getting up [either as soon as he awakens or at a particular time].
sn35.239:4.5 | S: In the last watch, they get up and continue to practice walking and sitting meditation, purifying their mind from obstacles. | T: During the last watch of the night [2 a.m. to dawn], sitting & pacing back & forth, he cleanses his mind of any qualities that would hold the mind in check.
sn35.239:4.6 | S: This is how a mendicant is committed to wakefulness. | T: “This is how a monk is one who is devoted to wakefulness.
sn35.239:4.7 | S: When a mendicant possesses these three qualities they’re full of joy and happiness in this very life, and they have laid the groundwork for ending the defilements.” | T: “Endowed with these three qualities, a monk dwells full of happiness & joy in the here & now, and has initiated a source for the ending of the effluents.”
sn35.239:4.8 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.241
sn35.241:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Kosambī on the bank of the Ganges River. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Ajjheya on the bank of the river Ganges.
sn35.241:1.2 | S: Seeing a large tree trunk being carried along by the current, | T: He saw a large log being carried along by the current of the river Ganges,
sn35.241:1.3 | S: he addressed the mendicants, | T: and on seeing it said to the monks:
sn35.241:1.4 | S: “Mendicants, do you see that large tree trunk being carried along by the current of the Ganges River?” | T: “Monks, do you see that large log being carried along by the current of the river Ganges?”
sn35.241:1.5 | S: “Yes, sir.” | T: “Yes, lord.”
sn35.241:1.6 | S: “Mendicants, assume that that tree trunk doesn’t drift toward the near shore or the far shore, or sink in the middle, or get stranded on high ground. And assume that it doesn’t get taken by humans or non-humans or caught up in a whirlpool, and that it doesn’t rot away. | T: “Monks, if that log doesn’t veer toward the near shore, doesn’t veer toward the far shore, doesn’t sink in the middle, doesn’t get washed up on high ground, doesn’t get snared by human beings, doesn’t get snared by non-human beings, doesn’t get snared in a whirlpool, and doesn’t become rotten inside,
sn35.241:1.7 | S: In that case, that tree trunk will slant, slope, and incline toward the ocean. | T: it will tend to the ocean, tilt to the ocean, incline to the ocean.
sn35.241:1.8 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn35.241:1.9 | S: Because the current of the Ganges River slants, slopes, and inclines toward the ocean. | T: Because the current of the river Ganges tends to the ocean, tilts to the ocean, inclines to the ocean.
sn35.241:2.1 | S: In the same way, assume that you don’t drift toward the near shore or the far shore, or sink in the middle, or get stranded on high ground. And assume that you don’t get taken by humans or non-humans or caught up in a whirlpool, and that you don’t rot away. | T: “In the same way, monks, if you don’t veer toward the near shore, don’t veer toward the far shore,
sn35.241:2.2 | P: na majjhe saṁsīdissatha, na thale ussīdissatha, na manussaggāho gahessati, na amanussaggāho gahessati, na āvaṭṭaggāho gahessati, na antopūtī bhavissatha; | T: don’t sink in the middle, don’t get washed up on high ground, don’t get snared by human beings, don’t get snared by non-human beings, don’t get snared in a whirlpool, and don’t become rotten inside,
sn35.241:2.3 | S: In that case, you will slant, slope, and incline toward extinguishment. | T: you will tend to unbinding, tilt to unbinding, incline to unbinding.
sn35.241:2.4 | S: Why is that? | T: Why is that?
sn35.241:2.5 | S: Because right view slants, slopes, and inclines toward extinguishment.” | T: Because right view tends to unbinding, tilts to unbinding, inclines to unbinding.”
sn35.241:2.6 | S: When he said this, one of the mendicants said to the Buddha: | T: When this was said, a certain monk addressed the Blessed One:
sn35.241:2.7 | S: “But sir, what’s the near shore and what’s the far shore? What’s sinking in the middle? What’s getting stranded on high ground? What’s getting taken by humans or non-humans? What’s getting caught up in a whirlpool? And what’s rotting away?” | T: “What, lord, is the near shore? What is the far shore? What is sinking in the middle? What is being washed up on high ground? What is being snared by human beings? What is being snared by non-human beings? What is being snared by a whirlpool? What is becoming rotten inside?”
sn35.241:3.1 | S: “‘The near shore’, mendicant, is a term for the six interior sense fields. | T: “‘The near shore,’ monks, stands for the six internal sense media.
sn35.241:3.2 | S: ‘The far shore’ is a term for the six exterior sense fields. | T: ‘The far shore’ stands for the six external sense media.
sn35.241:3.3 | S: ‘Sinking in the middle’ is a term for greed and relishing. | T: ‘Sinking in the middle’ stands for passion & delight.
sn35.241:3.4 | S: ‘Stranded on high ground’ is a term for the conceit ‘I am’. | T: ‘Being washed up on high ground’ stands for the conceit, ‘I am.’
sn35.241:4.1 | S: And what’s getting taken by humans? | T: “And what, monks, is being snared by human beings?
sn35.241:4.2 | S: It’s when someone mixes closely with laypeople, sharing their joys and sorrows—happy when they’re happy and sad when they’re sad—and getting themselves involved in their business. | T: There is the case where a monk lives entangled with householders, delighting with them and sorrowing with them, happy when they are happy, pained when they are in pain, taking on their affairs as his own duty.
sn35.241:4.3 | S: That’s called getting taken by humans. | T: This is called being snared by human beings.
sn35.241:5.1 | S: And what’s getting taken by non-humans? | T: “And what, monks, is being snared by non-human beings?
sn35.241:5.2 | S: It’s when someone leads the spiritual life wishing to be reborn in one of the orders of gods: ‘By this precept or observance or fervent austerity or spiritual practice, may I become one of the gods!’ | T: There is the case where a certain monk lives the holy life in hopes of a certain company of devas, (thinking,) ‘By means of this virtue or practice or austerity or holy life I will become one sort of deva or another.’
sn35.241:5.3 | S: That’s called getting taken by non-humans. | T: This is called being snared by non-human beings.
sn35.241:5.4 | S: ‘Caught up in a whirlpool’ is a term for the five kinds of sensual stimulation. | T: “‘Being snared by a whirlpool’ stands for the five strings of sensuality.
sn35.241:6.1 | S: And what’s rotting away? | T: “ And what, monks, is becoming rotten inside?
sn35.241:6.2 | S: It’s when some person is unethical, of bad qualities, filthy, with suspicious behavior, underhand, no true ascetic or spiritual practitioner—though claiming to be one—rotten inside, festering, and depraved. | T: There is the case where a certain monk is unprincipled, evil, unclean and suspect in his undertakings, hidden in his actions, not a contemplative though claiming to be one, not leading the holy life though claiming to do so, inwardly rotten, oozing with desire, filthy by nature.
sn35.241:6.3 | S: This is called ‘rotting away’.” | T: This is called becoming rotten inside.”
sn35.241:7.1 | S: Now at that time Nanda the cowherd was sitting not far from the Buddha. | T: Now at that time Nanda the cowherd was standing not far from the Blessed One.
sn35.241:7.2 | S: Then he said to the Buddha: | T: Then he said to the Blessed One,
sn35.241:7.3 | S: “I won’t drift toward the near shore or the far shore, or sink in the middle, or get stranded on high ground. And I won’t get taken by humans or non-humans or caught up in a whirlpool, and I won’t rot away. | T: “Lord, I don’t veer toward the near shore, I don’t veer toward the far shore, I won’t sink in the middle, I won’t get washed up on high ground, I won’t get snared by human beings, I won’t get snared by non-human beings, I won’t get snared in a whirlpool, and I won’t become rotten inside.
sn35.241:7.4 | S: Sir, may I receive the going forth, the ordination in the Buddha’s presence?” | T: It would be good, lord, if I could obtain the Going-forth, if I could obtain Acceptance [as a monk].
sn35.241:7.5 | S: “Well then, Nanda, return the cows to their owners.” | T: “In that case, Nanda, lead the cows back to their owners.”
sn35.241:7.6 | S: “Sir, the cows will go back by themselves, since they love their calves.” | T: “The cows will go back, lord, out of attachment for their calves.”
sn35.241:7.7 | S: “Still, Nanda, you should return them to their owners.” | T: “Lead the cows back to their owners, Nanda.”
sn35.241:7.8 | S: Then Nanda, after returning the cows to their owners, went up to the Buddha and said to him, | T: Then, having led the cows back to their owners, Nanda the cowherd went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, told him,
sn35.241:7.9 | S: “Sir, I have delivered the cows to their owners. | T: “The cows, lord, have been led back to their owners.
sn35.241:7.10 | S: May I receive the going forth, the ordination in the Buddha’s presence?” | T: Let me obtain the Going-forth in the Blessed One’s presence! Let me obtain Acceptance!”
sn35.241:7.11 | S: And the cowherd Nanda received the going forth, the ordination in the Buddha’s presence. | T: So Nanda the cowherd obtained the Going-forth in the Blessed One’s presence, he obtained Acceptance.
sn35.241:7.12 | S: Not long after his ordination, | T: And not long after his Acceptance—dwelling alone, secluded, heedful, ardent, & resolute—he in no long time entered & remained in the supreme goal of the holy life, for which clansmen rightly go forth from home into homelessness, directly knowing & realizing it for himself in the here & now. He knew: “Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for the sake of this world.”
sn35.241:7.13 | S: Venerable Nanda became one of the perfected. | T: And thus Ven. Nanda became another one of the arahants.
sn35.241:7.14 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.243
sn35.243:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Sakyans, near Kapilavatthu in the Banyan Tree Monastery. | T: I have heard that on one occasion the Blessed One was staying among the Sakyans at Kapilavatthu in the Banyan Park.
sn35.243:1.2 | S: Now at that time a new town hall had recently been constructed for the Sakyans of Kapilavatthu. It had not yet been occupied by an ascetic or brahmin or any human at all. | T: Now at that time a new reception hall had just been built by the Kapilavatthu Sakyans, and it had not yet been dwelled in by any contemplative, brahman, or anyone at all in human form.
sn35.243:1.3 | S: Then the Sakyans of Kapilavatthu went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him, | T: So the Kapilavatthu Sakyans went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down, sat to one side. As they were sitting there they said to him,
sn35.243:1.4 | S: “Sir, a new town hall has recently been constructed for the Sakyans of Kapilavatthu. It has not yet been occupied by an ascetic or brahmin or any human at all. | T: “Lord, a new reception hall has just been built by the Kapilavatthu Sakyans, and it has not yet been dwelled in by any contemplative, brahman, or anyone at all in human form.
sn35.243:1.5 | S: May the Buddha be the first to use it, | T: May the Blessed One be the first to use it.
sn35.243:1.6 | S: and only then will the Sakyans of Kapilavatthu use it. | T: When the Blessed One has used it first, the Kapilavatthu Sakyans will use it afterwards.
sn35.243:1.7 | S: That would be for the lasting welfare and happiness of the Sakyans of Kapilavatthu.” | T: That will be for their long-term welfare & happiness.”
sn35.243:1.8 | S: The Buddha consented with silence. | T: The Blessed One acquiesced with silence.
sn35.243:2.1 | S: Knowing that the Buddha had consented, the Sakyans rose from their seats, bowed and respectfully circled the Buddha, keeping him on their right. Then they went to the new town hall, where they spread carpets all over, prepared seats, set up a water jar, and placed an oil lamp. Then they went back to the Buddha and told him of their preparations, saying, | T: Sensing his acquiescence, the Kapilavatthu Sakyans got up from their seats, bowed down to him, circumambulated him, and then went to the new reception hall. On arrival, they spread it all over with felt rugs, arranged seats, set out a water vessel, and raised an oil lamp. Then they went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down, stood to one side. As they were standing there they said to him,
sn35.243:2.2 | P: “sabbasantharisanthataṁ, bhante, santhāgāraṁ, āsanāni paññattāni, udakamaṇiko patiṭṭhāpito, telappadīpo āropito. | T: “Lord, the reception hall has been covered all over with felt rugs, seats have been arranged, a water vessel has been set out, and an oil lamp raised.
sn35.243:2.3 | S: “Please, sir, come at your convenience.” | T: It is now time for the Blessed One to do as he sees fit.”
sn35.243:2.4 | S: Then the Buddha robed up and, taking his bowl and robe, went to the new town hall together with the Saṅgha of mendicants. Having washed his feet, he entered the town hall and sat against the central column facing east. | T: So the Blessed One—adjusting his lower robe and taking his bowl & outer robe—went together with a Saṅgha of monks to the reception hall. On arrival he washed his feet, entered the hall, and sat with his back to the central post, facing east.
sn35.243:2.5 | S: The Saṅgha of mendicants also washed their feet, entered the town hall, and sat against the west wall facing east, with the Buddha right in front of them. | T: The Saṅgha of monks washed their feet, entered the hall, and sat with their backs to the western wall, facing east, ranged around the Blessed One.
sn35.243:2.6 | S: The Sakyans of Kapilavatthu also washed their feet, entered the town hall, and sat against the east wall facing west, with the Buddha right in front of them. | T: The Kapilavatthu Sakyans washed their feet, entered the hall, and sat with their backs to the eastern wall, facing west, ranged around the Blessed One.
sn35.243:2.7 | S: The Buddha spent much of the night educating, encouraging, firing up, and inspiring the Sakyans with a Dhamma talk. Then he sent them off, saying, | T: Then the Blessed One—having spent most of the night instructing, urging, rousing, & encouraging the Kapilavatthu Sakyans with a Dhamma talk—dismissed them, saying,
sn35.243:2.8 | S: “The night is getting late, Gotamas. | T: “The night is far past, Gotamas.
sn35.243:2.9 | S: Please go at your convenience.” | T: Do what you now think it is time to do.”
sn35.243:2.10 | S: “Yes, sir,” replied the Sakyans. They got up from their seat, bowed and respectfully circled the Buddha, keeping him on their right, before leaving. | T: Responding, “As you say, lord,” to the Blessed One, the Kapilavatthu Sakyans rose from their seats, bowed down to the Blessed One, and—circumambulating him, keeping him to their right—departed.
sn35.243:3.1 | S: Then, not long after the Sakyans had left, the Buddha addressed Venerable Mahāmoggallāna, | T: Then not long after the Kapilavatthu Sakyans had left, the Blessed One addressed Ven. Mahā Moggallāna:
sn35.243:3.2 | S: “Moggallāna, the Saṅgha of mendicants is rid of dullness and drowsiness. | T: “Moggallāna, the Saṅgha of monks is free of drowsiness.
sn35.243:3.3 | S: Give them some Dhamma talk as you feel inspired. | T: Give them a Dhamma talk of your own devising.
sn35.243:3.4 | S: My back is sore, | T: My back aches.
sn35.243:3.5 | S: I’ll stretch it.” | T: I will rest it.”
sn35.243:3.6 | S: “Yes, sir,” Mahāmoggallāna replied. | T: “As you say, lord, Ven. Mahā Moggallāna responded to him.
sn35.243:3.7 | S: And then the Buddha spread out his outer robe folded in four and laid down in the lion’s posture—on the right side, placing one foot on top of the other—mindful and aware, and focused on the time of getting up. | T: Then the Blessed One, having arranged his outer robe folded in four, lay down on his right side in the lion’s sleeping posture, with one foot on top of the other, mindful & alert, having made a mental note to get up.
sn35.243:3.8 | S: There Venerable Mahāmoggallāna addressed the mendicants: | T: Then Ven. Mahā Moggallāna addressed the monks,
sn35.243:3.9 | S: “Reverends, mendicants!” | T: ”Friend monks!”
sn35.243:3.10 | S: “Reverend,” they replied. | T: “Yes, friend,” the monks responded to him.
sn35.243:3.11 | S: Venerable Mahāmoggallāna said this: | T: Ven. Mahā Moggallāna said,
sn35.243:3.12 | S: “I will teach you the explanation of the festering and the not festering. | T: ”Friends, I will teach you a discourse on being soggy and a discourse on not being soggy.
sn35.243:3.13 | S: Listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: Listen & pay careful attention. I will speak.”
sn35.243:3.14 | S: “Yes, reverend,” they replied. | T: “As you say, friend,” the monks responded to him.
