## sn35.247
sn35.247:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, suppose a person with wounded and festering limbs was to enter a thicket of reeds. | T: “Suppose that a man, wounded and festering, were to go into a swampy jungle.
sn35.247:1.2 | S: The kusa thorns would pierce their feet, and the reed leaves would scratch their limbs. | T: Its sharp-bladed grasses would pierce his feet; its thorns would scratch his festering sores.
sn35.247:1.3 | S: And that would cause that person to experience even more pain and distress. | T: And so, from that cause, he would experience an even greater measure of pain and unhappiness.
sn35.247:1.4 | S: In the same way, some mendicant goes to a village or a wilderness and gets scolded, | T: In the same way, there is the case where a certain monk, having gone to a village or to the wilderness, meets up with someone who upbraids him:
sn35.247:1.5 | S: ‘This venerable, acting like this, behaving like this, is a filthy village thorn.’ | T: ‘This venerable one, acting in this way, undertaking practices in this way, is a thorn of impurity in this village.’
sn35.247:1.6 | S: Understanding that they’re a thorn, they should understand restraint and lack of restraint. | T: Knowing this person to be a thorn, one should understand restraint and lack of restraint.
sn35.247:2.1 | S: And how is someone unrestrained? | T: “And what is lack of restraint?
sn35.247:2.2 | S: Take a mendicant who sees a sight with their eyes. If it’s pleasant they hold on to it, but if it’s unpleasant they dislike it. They live with mindfulness of the body unestablished and their heart stunted. | T: There is the case where a monk, seeing a form with the eye, is set on pleasing forms, is repelled by unpleasing forms, and remains with body-mindfulness unestablished, with limited awareness.
sn35.247:2.3 | S: And they don’t truly understand the freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom where those arisen bad, unskillful qualities cease without remainder. | T: He doesn’t discern, as it has come to be, the awareness-release, the discernment-release, where any evil, unskillful mental qualities that have arisen utterly cease without remainder.
sn35.247:2.4 | S: When they hear a sound with their ears … | T: “Hearing a sound with the ear…
sn35.247:2.5 | S: When they smell an odor with their nose … | T: “Smelling an aroma with the nose…
sn35.247:2.6 | S: When they taste a flavor with their tongue … | T: “Tasting a flavor with the tongue…
sn35.247:2.7 | S: When they feel a touch with their body … | T: “Touching a tactile sensation with the body…
sn35.247:2.8 | S: When they know an idea with their mind, if it’s pleasant they hold on to it, but if it’s unpleasant they dislike it. They live with mindfulness of the body unestablished and their heart stunted. | T: “Cognizing an idea with the intellect, he is set on pleasing ideas, is repelled by unpleasing ideas, and remains with body-mindfulness unestablished, with limited awareness.
sn35.247:2.9 | S: And they don’t truly understand the freedom of heart and freedom by wisdom where those arisen bad, unskillful qualities cease without remainder. | T: He doesn’t discern, as it has come to be, the awareness-release, the discernment-release, where any evil, unskillful mental qualities that have arisen utterly cease without remainder.
sn35.247:3.1 | S: Suppose a person was to catch six animals, with diverse territories and feeding grounds, and tie them up with a strong rope. | T: “Just as if a person, catching six animals of different ranges, of different habitats, were to bind them with a strong rope.
sn35.247:3.2 | S: They’d catch a snake, | T: Catching a snake, he would bind it with a strong rope.
sn35.247:3.3 | S: a dolphin, | T: Catching a crocodile…
sn35.247:3.4 | S: a bird, | T: a bird…
sn35.247:3.5 | S: a dog, | T: a dog…
sn35.247:3.6 | S: a jackal, | T: a hyena…
sn35.247:3.7 | S: and a monkey, | T: a monkey,
sn35.247:3.8 | S: tie each up with a strong rope, then tie a knot in the middle and let them loose. | T: he would bind it with a strong rope. Binding them all with a strong rope, and tying a knot in the middle, he would set chase to them.
sn35.247:3.9 | S: Then those six animals with different domains and ranges would each pull toward their own domain and range. | T: “Then those six animals, of different ranges, of different habitats, would each pull toward its own range & habitat.
sn35.247:3.10 | S: The snake would pull one way, thinking ‘I’m going into a termite mound!’ The dolphin would pull another way, thinking ‘I’m going into the water!’ The bird would pull another way, thinking ‘I’m flying into the atmosphere!’ The dog would pull another way, thinking ‘I’m going into the village!’ The jackal would pull another way, thinking ‘I’m going into the charnel ground!’ The monkey would pull another way, thinking ‘I’m going into the jungle!’ | T: The snake would pull, thinking, ‘I’ll go into the anthill.’ The crocodile would pull, thinking, ‘I’ll go into the water.’ The bird would pull, thinking, ‘I’ll fly up into the air.’ The dog would pull, thinking, ‘I’ll go into the village.’ The hyena would pull, thinking, ‘I’ll go into the charnel ground.’ The monkey would pull, thinking, ‘I’ll go into the forest.’
sn35.247:3.11 | S: When those six animals became exhausted and worn out, the strongest of them would get their way, and they’d all have to submit to their control. | T: And when these six animals became internally exhausted, they would submit, they would surrender, they would come under the sway of whichever among them was the strongest.
sn35.247:3.12 | S: In the same way, when a mendicant has not developed or cultivated mindfulness of the body, their eye pulls toward pleasant sights, but is put off by unpleasant sights. Their ear … nose … tongue … body … | T: In the same way, in any monk whose mindfulness immersed in the body is undeveloped & unpursued, the eye pulls toward pleasing forms, while unpleasing forms are repellent. The ear pulls toward pleasing sounds… The nose pulls toward pleasing aromas… The tongue pulls toward pleasing flavors… The body pulls toward pleasing tactile sensations…
sn35.247:3.13 | S: mind pulls toward pleasant ideas, but is put off by unpleasant ideas. | T: The intellect pulls toward pleasing ideas, while unpleasing ideas are repellent.
sn35.247:3.14 | S: This is how someone is unrestrained. | T: This, monks, is lack of restraint.
